{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdSNVsaJR3vdgJEnigXv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, and the self-check prints `OK`. Its ELPD-LOO on the short trial fit was −3492.4, below the incumbent's −3470.5. That number comes from one 100-draw chain, so it can't show whether the model would beat the incumbent. Its PSIS-LOO estimate was also flagged unreliable (7.7% of trials have Pareto k > 0.5, against a 1% tolerance). The check could not test convergence (R-hat needs more than one chain), and it does not test novelty. The full admission fit will settle all of these.\n\n**Model name:** `personal_decisiveness_complement_symmetry`\n\n**What it refines:** the incumbent, `complement_symmetry_aversion`.\n\n**The one change:** the incumbent lets each person have their own sensitivity to switching rate only. Here that sensitivity is shared by everyone. Instead, each person has an overall decisiveness, a scaling factor around 1 drawn from a population. It sharpens or flattens the whole comparison between the two sequences' impressions, every cue at once. So people differ in how consistently they follow the common impression of randomness.\n\n**Why:** the critique found that people differ in how often they agree with the majority choice more than the incumbent predicts (the only discrepancy that survived the FDR correction, q = 0.048). The incumbent's person-level terms can't produce that spread. Every other part of the incumbent is unchanged: the asymmetric tolerance around the personal ideal switching rate, the personal weights on imbalance and the longest streak, all the shared cue weights including complement symmetry, and the personal guessing rate.\n\n**Risk:** `personal_decisiveness_mirror_streak_lapse` also added a personal decisiveness, but it kept a personal switching sensitivity too, and it sits 294 nats behind. This version swaps the personal sensitivity out rather than stacking both. It still keeps personal weights on imbalance and the longest streak, which may partly trade off against the decisiveness scale in the full fit.\n\nFiles are in `experiment3/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_4/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2713,"cache_read_input_tokens":87461,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2713,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6ac9792-9179-4746-be24-f102e24a914d","uuid":"549d7aee-c8e1-44e7-9489-b9c9ccb0f534","timestamp":"2026-10-02T12:49:42.948Z","request_id":"req_011CfdSNVU2kBrCzLMJNh4Kn"}
